{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-130456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of candies selected with replacement from a bag before selecting a strawberry-flavored candy.\n- The number of messages received by a person before receiving a message from their boss\n- The number of strawberry flavored candies in a bag" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Selecting a candy and checking if it's a strawberry candy is a binomial experiment, and the number of candies selected before the first strawberry candy is selected can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Receiving a message and checking if it's from the boss is a binomial experiment, and the number of messages received before receiving the first message from the boss can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The number of candies (of some flavor) in a bag is not a binomial experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of people examined at a clinic during a day\n- The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city\n- The number of people examined at a clinic until a person infected with a certain virus is found" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, but the number of people examined cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The average age of the people infected with a virus in a certain city is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Being examined is a binomial experiment, and the number of people examined until a person infected with a certain virus is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times we draw a red card from a $52$-card deck\n- The number of people interviewed about whether they have a dog until one person says they have a dog\n- The number of cards that are selected from a deck with replacement before an ace is drawn" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Drawing a card from a deck is a binomial experiment, but the number of times we draw a red card cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Interviewing a person about whether or not they have a dog is a binomial experiment, and the number of people interviewed before the first person with a dog is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Drawing a card from a deck with replacement is a binomial experiment, and the number of cards selected from a deck with replacement before the first ace is drawn can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of ice creams sold at an ice cream shop before a chocolate flavor ice cream is sold\n- The price of a cone at an ice cream shop\n- The number of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Checking if a sold ice cream is chocolate is a binomial experiment, and the number of ice cream sold before the first chocolate ice cream flavor is sold can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The price of a cone of ice cream is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The number of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop is not a binomial experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{50}=0.08.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.08.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.08).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.08)^{x-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{x-1}(0.08)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{5-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{4}(0.08) \\\\  & ≈0.057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.038"] ["math" "0.057"] ["math" "0.081"] ["math" "0.102"] ["math" "0.125"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.057"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A customer service representative resolves a caller’s issue with a $45%$ chance during each minute of a call. The call continues until the issue is resolved. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ minutes to resolve the issue is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of minutes until the representative resolves the issue. Since the chance of resolving the issue in any given minute is $45%,$ the number of minutes until the issue is resolved can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=45%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.45)^{x-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{x-1}(0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the representative will resolve the issue for the first time in the $5$th minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{5-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{4}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332311" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332311" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.041"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-276119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A fair six-sided die is repeatedly rolled until the number six appears. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will be rolled exactly $2$ times is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-276119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rolls until a $6$ came on. The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of rolls until a $6$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{6}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a $6$ on the $2$nd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ≈0.139,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-276119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-276119" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-276119" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.139"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.012"] ["math" "0.079"] ["math" "0.105"] ["math" "0.316"] ["math" "0.422"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.105"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A bag contains $10$ balls numbered $1$ through $10.$ One ball is randomly selected, its number is recorded, and then it is placed back in the bag before the next selection. The process is repeated until the ball numbered $10$ is selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ selections is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came on. The probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on any single selection is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{10}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on the $5$th selection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{5-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{4}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332319" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332319" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.066"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.84"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company monitors incoming emails for spam. On average, $18$ out of every $150$ emails are spam messages. The emails are checked one by one until a spam email is found. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that a spam email is found before checking the $4$th email is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of emails checked until a spam email is found. On average, the chance that any single email is spam is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{150}=0.12.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of emails that are checked before finding a spam email can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.12.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.12).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.12)^{x-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{x-1}(0.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a spam email before checking $4$ emails is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{1-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{2-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{3-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.12)+(0.88)(0.12)+(0.88)^{2}(0.12) \\\\  & =[1+0.88+(0.88)^{2}](0.12) \\\\  & ≈0.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332342" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332342" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.319"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.256"] ["math" "0.287"] ["math" "0.332"] ["math" "0.386"] ["math" "0.405"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.386"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A card is being randomly picked from a standard $52$ cards deck and put back in until getting a red card. What is the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards picked until a red one is chosen. Since there are $26$ red cards in a $52$ cards deck, the chance of choosing a red card is $\\frac{26}{52}.$ Thus, the number of chosen cards before getting a red card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{26}{52}=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{1-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{2-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{5-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)+(0.5)(0.5)+(0.5)^{2}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & =[1+0.5+(0.5)^{2}+(0.5)^{3}+(0.5)^{4}](0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.469"] ["math" "0.492"] ["math" "0.938"] ["math" "0.969"] ["math" "0.984"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.969"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.292"] ["math" "0.417"] ["math" "0.990"] ["math" "0.992"] ["math" "0.994"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-130789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.992"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $5%,$ the number of sentences checked until the first typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=5%=\\frac{5}{100}=0.05.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.05).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.05)^{x-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{x-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require $P(X\\ge 4).$ \n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{1-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{2-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{3-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.05)+(0.95)(0.05)+(0.95)^{2}(0.05) \\\\  & =[1+0.95+(0.95)^{2}](0.05) \\\\  & ≈0.143,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, there are no typos within the first three sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.143 \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.702"] ["math" "0.751"] ["math" "0.773"] ["math" "0.825"] ["math" "0.857"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-85282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.857"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A parcel delivery company records whether each delivered package requires a signature upon arrival. On average, $9$ out of $50$ packages require a signature. Each package's status is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first package requiring a signature is the fifth package delivered or later is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of packages delivered until a package that requires a signature is delivered. On average, the chance that any single package requires a signature is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{50}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of packages until the first package that requires a signature is delivered can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.18.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.18)}^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & ={(0.82)}^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{4-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.18)+(0.82)(0.18)+(0.82)^{2}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+0.82+(0.82)^{2}+(0.82)^{3}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.548,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that at least $4$ packages are delivered before the first package that requires a signature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.548 \\\\  & ≈0.452,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332361" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.452"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A board game uses a special deck of $10$ cards: $4$ cards show a star and $6$ cards show a circle. A player randomly draws a card, records the symbol, and then puts the card back into the deck before drawing again. The player keeps drawing until they get a star card. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards drawn until a star card is obtained. The chance of selecting a star card from the deck is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections before obtaining a star card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{9}{25}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.216"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A coffee shop tracks how often customers order a latte. On average, $30%$ of customers order a latte. Each customer’s order is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers served until a latte is ordered. On average, the chance that any single customer orders a latte is \n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of customers before the first latte order can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.3.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.3)}^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & ={(0.7)}^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{3-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3)+(0.7)^{2}(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7+(0.7)^{2}](0.3) \\\\  & =0.657.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.657 \\\\  & =0.343.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332356" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332356" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.343"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "q-332355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.833"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "339b60716252a836fee9506decf5ae24b65bf62a4ae6a4e83f155ce4e75ba01b"} {"question" "e-7500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\n- The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\n- The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7500.json" "file_sha256" "9db5f1a8139e31c66bd2b64245d9c6e36d2e09761cc33be45c0f74f12e745444"} {"question" "e-7500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7500.json" "file_sha256" "9db5f1a8139e31c66bd2b64245d9c6e36d2e09761cc33be45c0f74f12e745444"} {"question" "e-7501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An archer is practicing archery. On average, the archer has a $7\\%$ chance of missing every shot he makes. What is the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7501.json" "file_sha256" "0405ec5e347138e1e36e5a2cba5242b393ee367e505f56f030c81a498c05c212"} {"question" "e-7501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%,$ the number of shots  until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.07)^{x-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{x-1}(0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{8-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\  & ≈0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7501.json" "file_sha256" "0405ec5e347138e1e36e5a2cba5242b393ee367e505f56f030c81a498c05c212"} {"question" "e-7502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A high school soccer team is playing a local tournament. On average, the team has a $10\\%$ chance of getting a tie in every game. What is the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7502.json" "file_sha256" "dca07a7a6a45e2ec86395c224dabec96851556d20abe0a1a88b275abb62c0f04"} {"question" "e-7502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7502.json" "file_sha256" "dca07a7a6a45e2ec86395c224dabec96851556d20abe0a1a88b275abb62c0f04"} {"question" "e-7503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company is conducting interviews to fill a job. On average, $35\\%$ of applicants meet all the requirements. What is the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7503.json" "file_sha256" "dbe737bb8265373131ec7f06dd12c8b05953aa963af5777c9e5a15a21a6cbe88"} {"question" "e-7503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/example-7503.json" "file_sha256" "dbe737bb8265373131ec7f06dd12c8b05953aa963af5777c9e5a15a21a6cbe88"}] "usage" {"e-7500" [] "q-85282" [] "q-85272" [] "e-7501" [] "e-7502" [] "e-7503" [] "q-85276" []} "basis" 2428 "retractions" [[17592186157805 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAt a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157806 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157808 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157051 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n1. The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n2. The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n3. The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head"] [17592186157051 :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186157052 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%$, the number of shots until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.07)^{x - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{x - 1}(0.07)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 8) &= f(8) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{8 - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.042\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157053 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%$, the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.1)^{x - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{x - 1}(0.1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{1 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{2 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{3 - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.1) + (0.9)(0.1) + (0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.9 + (0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.271\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186157054 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%$, the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.35)^{x - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{x - 1}(0.35)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3)$, we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{1 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{2 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{3 - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.35) + (0.65)(0.35) + (0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.65 + (0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.725\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 3) &= 1 - P(X \\le 3) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.725 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.275\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156083 17592186156084 17592186156085 17592186156086 17592186156087 17592186156089 17592186156090 17592186156091 17592186156092 17592186156093 17592186156101 17592186156102 17592186156103 17592186156104 17592186156105] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156082 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156082 :answer-field/id] [17592186156082 :answer-field/key] [17592186156082 :answer-field/type] [17592186156088 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156088 :answer-field/id] [17592186156088 :answer-field/key] [17592186156088 :answer-field/type] [17592186156100 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156100 :answer-field/id] [17592186156100 :answer-field/key] [17592186156100 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
